WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.940
Google just sent an ai prototype into orbit to test running smart computers in space. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 185. Picture a helper on your laptop that can open every folder you own. Apple is adding new limits to a Mac permission called Full Disk Access, R's Technica Reports. A Mac is Apple's computer, and Full Disk Access is like handing an app the master key to the whole house. With that key, an app can reach every file

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:36.000
message, email, and browsing record, without asking again and again. Apple announced the

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.180
change on October 2nd after seeing how capable these tools have become with private data.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.960
The tools in question are AI agents, which are artificial intelligence programs built to

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.640
finish multi-step jobs on their own. Think of an intern who does not just answer one question,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:58.480
but walks the office and handles a whole stack of tasks. Sorting documents or summarizing

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.660
long chats are the kind of jobs these agents try to complete.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.800
Because those jobs often touch private data, the agent often asks for that broad master

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.780
key permission so it can work smoothly.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.220
Here is the risk.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.000
A poorly designed or misused agent could copy sensitive information or make unintended

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
changes across the entire system.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.400
That is less like a spell checker fixing one sentence, and more like someone rearranging

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.900
every drawer in your bedroom.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:31.300
Apple is adding extra confirmation steps and clearer warnings, so the permission is

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.740
granted only when you truly mean to allow it.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.980
The new rules are like a second lock on the front door, plus a sign that says, you are

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.680
about to give away the only key.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:46.500
This update matters to anyone who uses a Mac and cares about privacy, especially as

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.540
more everyday apps add AI helpers that promise to do things for you.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.520
Mac users will start seeing the new prompts right away when an app asks for broad

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.860
access. You can check this yourself in about 30 seconds for free on the computer you already have.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.720
Open system settings, click privacy and security, then scroll to full disk access.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:13.260
Look at the list and turn off any app you no longer trust. If a name looks unfamiliar,

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.620
that is your cue to pause. The same way you would pause before lending a stranger your house keys.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.920
A robot vacuum makes the next part easier to see. A vacuum only cleans well if it knows where

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.520
where the furniture sits and which doors are open or it gets stuck.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.420
Now stretch that picture.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.500
The vacuum can also open drawers, read your notes, and decide what to throw away.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.360
An AI agent works in a similar chain.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:44.240
You give it a goal, such as, organize my photos, and it tries to finish the whole job, not

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:44.860
one tap.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.760
Step 1 is the goal.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.280
You say what you want, the way you tell a friend, please tidy the living room.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.100
Step 2 is the map.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.300
the agent looks across many files and apps because a big job rarely lives in a

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:04.900
single folder. Step 3 is action. It sorts, summarizes, moves, or replies, taking

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:10.500
several steps in a row without you clicking each one. Step 4 is permission. Someone

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.960
has to say yes before it can open those drawers, or it might wander into places

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.700
it should not. The extra apple locks are there so the helpful robot stays

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.340
helpful, instead of accidentally making a mess.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:28.180
Once you see an agent as a helper that needs a map and a key, the whole idea sits still

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:28.720
in your head.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.620
Planet Labs helped Google build a small satellite to see how its AI chips handle the extreme

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.700
conditions of orbit.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:41.800
The satellite went up on SpaceX's Transporter 18 Ridershare mission, which is a bit like

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.100
carpooling on a rocket.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.800
Many small payloads share one launch, the way several classmates share one van to a field

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.120
trip. On board are Google TPUs, special chips of the same kind used in

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.880
data centers to train and run large models. A data center is a giant building

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.080
packed with computers. These chips are the engines that help chat bots and

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.420
image tools think. The goal is to gather data on how the chips survive the

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.540
physical stress and temperature swings of space flight. Picture packing your

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.780
gaming laptop for a camping trip, where it is freezing at night and blazing at

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.300
noon, then asking whether it still works.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.180
One big advantage of low Earth orbit, a path relatively close to the planet, is near

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.220
constant sunlight.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.780
The project says that sunlight could generate up to 8 times more solar power than systems

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:31.420
on the ground.

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.160
That is like a solar panel that almost never waits for night.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.340
This is still early research.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.400
It shows one possible future where some of the heavy computing that powers chatbots and

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.900
image tools might happen above the clouds instead of in giant data centers.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:53.280
If you are into space, climate tech, or big what if ideas, this project is worth following.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.520
Here is a free try this you can do on your phone in a minute, with no account required.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:05.560
Open X, search Google AI, and look for the post from October 2 about Project Suncatcher.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.580
Read the short announcement and notice what they chose to measure first.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.940
Chips under stress, not a finished space chatbot.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:18.540
Ten popular chatbots recommended unlicensed gambling sites, a Dutch report found.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:26.580
Researchers asked chat GPT, Gemini, Claude, Grock, and six other chatbots questions in Dutch about casinos.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.640
Every single one named at least one site that does not have a proper Dutch license.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:37.680
The study, run by Kermeric Verontwurda affiliates, tested nine questions per tool.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.220
Some chatbots even presented missing identity checks or large bonuses as advantages.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.760
The watchdog warned that Dutch consumers should be able to trust accurate information when

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:50.760
they ask about legal gambling options.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:55.540
Treat chatbot answers about money and rules like a stranger's restaurant tip.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:57.820
Check the official list before you go.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.220
Christopher Ola, one of the people who started Anthropic, has been quietly meeting with

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:03.920
religious leaders.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:10.380
They have been discussing whether the company's AI model Claude could someday feel pain or have

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.200
experiences that matter morally.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:18.620
The conversations began in the fall of 2025 as part of an effort to think about AI ethics

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:19.120
early.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:25.660
That is happening while the company pushes toward a $2 trillion valuation and an eventual IPO,

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.840
which is when a company starts selling shares to the public.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:34.280
Critics have noted that treating the model as a possible moral entity could affect how

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.000
the company handles liability if something goes wrong.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.480
It is a hard, honest question.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.920
More like asking whether a very convincing character in a game can actually get hurt and

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.260
people do not agree yet.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:47.700
That's it for today.

